\section{Reading the negbin community dispersion}{

Under \code{mixture = "negbin"} the community mean \code{mu_log_r} reaches
\code{coef()} / \code{vcov()} / \code{confint()} with a marginal Wald SE, and the variance
component \code{sigma_log_r} is reported on \code{fit$ms_dispersion}. That interval is
calibrated \strong{conditional on the variance component being recovered}, and the
two are not independent: \code{sigma_log_r} is a scalar variance over species and
at few species it can settle near its lower boundary, which shifts
\code{mu_log_r} and narrows its SE at the same time.

Measured on simulated data (39 \code{laplace} fits at 8 and 36 species,
\code{sigma_logr = 0.5}): where \code{sigma_log_r} came back at
least 0.30, the nominal 95\% interval covered 33 of 34 with a
\code{sqrt(mean(z^2))} of 0.88; where it came back below, it covered 2 of 5, the
point estimate was 2.2x further from the truth and the SE 28\% narrower. So
read \code{fit$ms_dispersion$sigma_log_r} before quoting a \code{mu_log_r} interval: a
value near zero, well under what the data should support, means the interval
is not trustworthy even though the fit converged and reported no warning.
Those thresholds are the ones this fixture separated on and are not a general
rule; what transfers is the conditioning, not the number.

Away from that boundary the interval is calibrated, and calibrated at every
group count measured. Over 97 fits at 8 / 18 / 36 species the scale
\code{sd(mu_log_r) / mean(se)} reads 1.066 / 1.265 / 0.981, and the middle figure
is the seed block rather than the estimator: \code{mu_log_r} is a POPULATION mean,
each seed draws \code{S} log-dispersions around it, and that draw supplies about
two thirds of the across-seed spread. The 18-species blocks drew theirs
18-21\% wider than \code{sigma_logr / sqrt(S)}. Put the draw at its expectation and
the scale is 1.077 / 1.101 / 0.977; rebuild the SE at the simulated sigma as
well and it is 0.990 / 1.035 / 0.963 (\code{gcol33/tulpaObs#285},
NOTES_measurements.md). The residual there is the attenuation of
\code{sigma_log_r} itself (0.418 / 0.448 / 0.487 against 0.5), which shrinks as
species are added and which the SE inherits, since
\code{se^2 = (sigma_log_r^2 + c) / S}.

A simulation study measuring this needs \code{simulate_ms_abun()}'s
\code{truth$mu_log_r_real} -- the mean of the log-dispersions that seed actually
drew -- beside \code{truth$mu_log_r}. Score coverage of a community mean against
the constant; score point recovery and interval SCALE against the realized
mean, or the draw is charged to the estimator.

\code{control$logr.sigma.prior} puts a Penalized-Complexity prior on that variance,
which adds curvature at \code{sigma_log_r -> 0}. Measured on the same fixture at
\code{c(1, 0.05)}, it is worth reaching for when a fit reports a \code{sigma_log_r} near
zero or fails outright with a singular marginal Hessian -- one seed that
errored under pure maximum likelihood converged and covered under the prior,
and variance components near 0.01-0.06 lifted by an order of magnitude. It
does \strong{not} repair coverage: on 19 paired seeds the nominal 95\% interval
covered 17 either way, with the same two seeds missing, because the prior
bites near the boundary while those misses sit at \code{sigma_log_r} around 0.2.
It is off by default because the fits that were already calibrated pick up a
small systematic shift under it (\code{mu_log_r} by -0.006, p = 0.001).
